<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lavaux</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lavaux is a region of terraced vineyards on the north shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-geneva">Lake Geneva</a> in the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a> and Vevey. The terraces were built from the 11th century under monasteries and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Lausanne, who owned much of the land. Lavaux was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2007.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lavaux.jpg" alt="Lavaux" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lorenz Poffet · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lavaux_Switzerland.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
